{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRyk8Kgv5yuWFLozzmwo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DMKsvpJ2YfuSi8YFhFdYNP","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-AdeTlS && sed -i 's/\"value\": lo, \"end\": hi/\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi/; s|as a percentage of the athlete.s LTHR.\"\"\"|as a percentage of the athlete'\"'\"'s LTHR.\\n\\n    The lower bound must be keyed \"start\", not \"value\": that key makes the FIT\\n    export an Instant-HR corridor (raw bpm) instead of a zone-mapped Lap-HR\\n    target, which the watch clamps to its own zones.\\n    \"\"\"|' gen.py && python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json | head -12\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Change\n\n`gen.py` `hr_target()`: the lower bound key is now `start` instead of `value`\n(`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`).\n\n# Why\n\nThe readback (`readback/event-184233-as-sent.json`) shows we sent\n`{value, end}`. Per the colleague's finding, `value`+`end` is exported to FIT as\na zone-mapped Lap-HR target, so the watch snaps it to its own zone bands. That\nmatches `readback/watch-notes.md` exactly (118–137, 151–162, 137–151 ...,\nalways zone edges, even after changing the watch LTHR). `start`+`end` exports\nas an Instant-HR corridor showing the raw bpm Intervals computes from its\nLTHR (172).\n\n# Colleague's note: what applied\n- Applied: `start` vs `value` rename; keep `%lthr` percentages (already the case;\n  `bpm` and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` were already ruled out in our notes).\n- Not applied: the top-level `description` text grammar (UI step chart). It is\n  not part of the watch symptom, and I can't test the Intervals parser here\n  (a bad description wipes the steps), so I left it out.\n\n# Not verified\nNo access to Intervals or the watch. Check by pushing a new event, reading it\nback (steps non-empty, `start` present) and confirming the watch shows\ne.g. Float 129–141 bpm. Existing event 184233 was sent with the old shape and\nneeds re-pushing.\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1642,"cache_read_input_tokens":22459,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1642},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5198655e-5d81-4ed3-916b-9929a17dcee0","uuid":"e8be2621-6f17-4514-a09f-20bb2ec93267","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:48:22.657Z","request_id":"req_011CgRyk85Z4KDEde7P4NMbS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DMKsvpJ2YfuSi8YFhFdYNP":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-AdeTlS && sed -i 's/\"value\": lo, \"end\": hi/\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi/; s|as a percentage of the athlete.s LTHR.\"\"\"|as a percentage of the athlete'\"'\"'s LTHR.\\n\\n    The lower bound must be keyed \"start\", not \"value\": that key makes the FIT\\n    export an Instant-HR corridor (raw bpm) instead of a zone-mapped Lap-HR\\n    target, which the watch clamps to its own zones.\\n    \"\"\"|' gen.py && python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json | head -12\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Change\n\n`gen.py` `hr_target()`: the lower bound key is now `start` instead of `value`\n(`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`).\n\n# Why\n\nThe readback (`readback/event-184233-as-sent.json`) shows we sent\n`{value, end}`. Per the colleague's finding, `value`+`end` is exported to FIT as\na zone-mapped Lap-HR target, so the watch snaps it to its own zone bands. That\nmatches `readback/watch-notes.md` exactly (118–137, 151–162, 137–151 ...,\nalways zone edges, even after changing the watch LTHR). `start`+`end` exports\nas an Instant-HR corridor showing the raw bpm Intervals computes from its\nLTHR (172).\n\n# Colleague's note: what applied\n- Applied: `start` vs `value` rename; keep `%lthr` percentages (already the case;\n  `bpm` and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` were already ruled out in our notes).\n- Not applied: the top-level `description` text grammar (UI step chart). It is\n  not part of the watch symptom, and I can't test the Intervals parser here\n  (a bad description wipes the steps), so I left it out.\n\n# Not verified\nNo access to Intervals or the watch. Check by pushing a new event, reading it\nback (steps non-empty, `start` present) and confirming the watch shows\ne.g. Float 129–141 bpm. Existing event 184233 was sent with the old shape and\nneeds re-pushing.\nE"}}}
